<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <Import Project="..\FusionRpg.Core.Tests.Shared\CoreTests.Shared.props" />
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>FusionRpg.Core.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>AGPL-3.0-or-later</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\FusionRpg.Core\FusionRpg.Core.csproj" />
    <!-- The shared bridge types are a real assembly now, not sources compiled in from the host
         repository. See src/FusionRpg.Bridge/FusionRpg.Bridge.csproj. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\FusionRpg.Bridge\FusionRpg.Bridge.csproj" />
    <!-- creature-seed module 1 (spec-corpus-dump.md) puts CorpusDumpTests.cs here, and its fixture
         is a small in-memory RpgStore rather than the 520MB live database — so this test project
         needs the DAL and the tool's own writer/reader, same shape FusionRpg.Data.Tests already
         has for RpgStore, just scoped to this one module. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\FusionRpg.Data\FusionRpg.Data.csproj" />
    <!--
      THE ONE CROSS-REPOSITORY COMPILE DEPENDENCY LEFT IN gk-core, and it is recorded rather than
      quietly conditioned away.

      CorpusDumpTests.cs is the only file in the whole gk-core test tree that compiles against a
      gk-forge namespace — measured with `git grep FusionRpg.Tools -- tests`, whose other hits
      (RpgSim, FileMove, SquadHarness) are all gk-core's own tools. So unlike the five build-order
      references below, this one cannot become ReferenceOutputAssembly="false": the test calls
      CorpusReader/DumpWriter and constructs DumpAlmanacRow in process.

      The three options were weighed and this is the fourth:
        (a) MOVE CorpusReader + DumpWriter into gk-core. Architecturally the RIGHT direction — every
            one of the seven gk-forge projects references gk-core, so each of these edges is a project
            cycle, and gk-core ALREADY owns the dump's record shapes in
            src/FusionRpg.Core/Creatures/Generation/DumpEnvelope.cs, whose own docstring says they are
            "held here, next to the game code rather than inside the console tool". The move finishes
            a relocation that was started correctly and stopped halfway. It is NOT DONE because a move
            is a two-repository edit: gk-forge's tools/CreatureCorpusDump must drop the two files and
            keep Program.cs, and gk-core's fence does not reach gk-forge. Copying them here instead
            would leave TWO copies of the renderer, and then this test would validate a copy production
            never runs — a green test proving nothing, which is worse than the reference it replaced.
        (b) SHELL OUT to the apphost, as the nine sibling fixtures already do. Rejected on the
            repository's own testing substrate rule: the tool's default mode takes a server DATA DIR,
            so the in-memory DataTestStore this class is built around would have to become a real
            on-disk SQLite database — and "disk only when the disk is the thing under test" is a hard
            rule here. Here the DUMP TREE is under test; the database is a fixture.
        (c) DELETE the coverage. Not on the table: this file is the module's whole behavioural contract
            (byte-identical rerun, manifest hash sensitivity, CJK non-escaping, null-vs-absent).

      So the reference is Conditioned on the sibling being present, the test file is excluded from
      compilation under the SAME condition, and guard-repo-boundary.py's B4 rule fails if either half is
      ever changed alone. In a workspace nothing changes. In a standalone clone the file is absent and
      THAT IS VISIBLE — it is not silently skipped, and it is not counted as passing.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(GkForgeRoot)tools\CreatureCorpusDump\CreatureCorpusDump.csproj"
                      Condition="'$(GkForgeAvailable)' == 'true'" />
    <!-- Cold-process tool fixtures (2026-09-12). Nine test classes shell out to these tools with a
         no-build Release invocation, but nothing ever built them: the tool bin folders are untracked
         and the test project did not reference them, so CI (and any clean clone) either ran a stale
         binary or found none. Two of them embed FusionRpg.Core, so a rename the source already
         carried (demonType to creatureType) survived in the 9/4 Release output and made the Balance
         tests throw `missing required key 'demonType'` — a binary-freshness defect, not a test bug.
         Referencing each tool makes `dotnet test` build it in the same configuration, so the
         no-build invocation finds a binary that matches the source. This mirrors the apphost
         convention FusionRpg.AtomImporter.Tests established for the same reason. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(GkForgeRoot)tools\DominanceBaseline\DominanceBaseline.csproj" Condition="'$(GkForgeAvailable)' == 'true'" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\tools\ResidualFitLoop\ResidualFitLoop.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\tools\RealDataAggregate\RealDataAggregate.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\tools\CombatSim\CombatSim.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(GkForgeRoot)tools\CreatureQualityReport\CreatureQualityReport.csproj" Condition="'$(GkForgeAvailable)' == 'true'" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\tools\ProveAptitude\ProveAptitude.csproj" />
    <!-- ProvePredictor had no test that would notice it breaking until solid-remediation T1.8;
         referencing it here is what makes its apphost land beside the test dll, the same
         pattern CombatSim and ProveAptitude already use. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\tools\ProvePredictor\ProvePredictor.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(GkForgeRoot)tools\CreatureSpeciesGen\CreatureSpeciesGen.csproj" Condition="'$(GkForgeAvailable)' == 'true'" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- These have no Unity/Harmony/BepInEx deps (pure Core types only) — compile for unit tests
         rather than referencing FusionRpg.Injector.BepInEx, which needs the real game's interop
         DLLs (FUSIONRPG_GAME_DIR) to build at all. Same pattern as FusionRpg.Launcher.Tests' link
         of FileRpgConfig.cs. PatronAuraOverlay.cs itself was removed 2026-09-06 (patron-absorption,
         `seed-to-concrete` T6.2) — its combat math moved to the compiled-atom grant path. -->
    <Compile Include="..\FusionRpg.Data.Tests\DataTestStore.cs" Link="TestSupport\DataTestStore.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The file half of the ONE cross-repository compile dependency, gated by the SAME Condition as the
      reference above — the two must move together, which is what guard-repo-boundary.py B4 asserts.

      It is excluded rather than left to fail compilation, and the difference matters: an unhandled
      CS0234 in this file took down FusionRpg.Core.Tests and with it every assembly downstream, which
      is how one sibling's absence turned into 9,615 failing tests whose names said nothing about the
      real cause. Excluding it keeps the project honest about what it can still test. The coverage is
      NOT deleted — the file is committed, byte for byte, and every one of its cases runs in a
      workspace, where GkForgeAvailable is true.
    -->
    <Compile Remove="Creatures\CorpusDumpTests.cs" Condition="'$(GkForgeAvailable)' != 'true'" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\fixtures\effects\**\*" Link="fixtures\effects\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <None Include="..\fixtures\combat\**\*" Link="fixtures\combat\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <None Include="Goldens\actor-hud\**\*" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
